  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until Salas ended it in the tenth.",
    "what_this_means": "Ethan Salas walked it off with a single, but the game was won earlier. In the third inning when the Padres clawed back from down four, and in the ninth when they wouldn't let Miami slip away. This is a team that doesn't need to lead early. They scored on four of twelve RISP opportunities, converting when it mattered most. Ty France went 3-for-5 with an RBI, and the offense found enough at the right moments. After Miami took a three-run lead in the second, the Padres answered with three of their own in the third, then stayed level through a tied sixth, seventh, eighth, and ninth. The bullpen held firm after Casey Mize exited after three innings. Randy V\u00e1squez threw two scoreless, and the back end bridged to extra innings without breaking. Salas's tenth-inning single was the punctuation, but it was the balance all night that made it possible. Late-inning rallies and clutch role players: this is still the formula working.",
